# CMake build directories. The leading slash is load-bearing, not style: without it the
# pattern matches at *every* level, and `build-*/` quietly excluded two source directories
# three levels down as well -- .agents/skills/build-verify/ and .agents/skills/building-data-apps/.
# Neither had a single tracked file. Anchoring keeps the rule to the repository root, where
# the build trees actually are.
/build/
/build-*/
# build-review/ is the verification suite's source directory, not a build tree, but
# `build-*` cannot tell the difference -- and it matched this one too. Every probe source
# and the build script sat untracked for the project's entire history because of it. The
# negation has to come after the glob it corrects, and git only re-includes the directory
# because its parent (the repository root) is not itself excluded. Check it with:
#     git check-ignore -v build-review/probe_partial_repaint.cpp
!build-review/
# The PNGs the render scaffolds write. They are regenerated, never edited, and the
# render_evidence target reproduces them from source -- binary churn in the history buys
# nothing. The probe sources and the shell scripts stay tracked.
build-review/*.png
CMakeCache.txt
CMakeFiles/
CMakeScripts/
Makefile
cmake_install.cmake
install_manifest.txt
compile_commands.json

# Deployment output: the staged folder and the installer tools/deploy.sh produces.
# Regenerate with `bash tools/deploy.sh --build`; nothing here is source.
dist/

# Qt specifics
*.moc
moc_*.cpp
*.qrc.depends
*.qrc.o
qrc_*.cpp
*.ui
ui_*.h

# Local tooling caches
.cache/

# Qt Creator template leftovers (real entry point is src/main.cpp)
/main.cpp
/Main.qml
/importedcontent/
/*_output.txt
/err.txt
/out.txt

# IDE and OS
.vs/
.idea/
.vscode/
*.user
*.suo
*.sln
*.vcxproj
*.vcxproj.filters
*.xcodeproj/
*.xcworkspace/
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
